# Verdict

<!--
Written for an agent to act on, not for a human to enjoy reading: be
precise and evidence-backed, not literary.

Every verdict needs a reasonable "why" — cite what you actually found
investigating, don't just assert a conclusion. This matters most when the
verdict is ITERATE: this document becomes the next Optimizer's starting
point, so it must contain reasons and evidence clear enough that a fresh
agent, who was not here, understands exactly what was wrong with this
round's conclusion or approach — not merely that something was wrong.

On PASS, `code disposition` needs the same standard of reasoning: `revert`
is not the automatic default just because end-to-end showed no gain —
`keep` is legitimate when there's real evidence the code is worth having
anyway (a unit-test-level win, a sound case for other input shapes). Say
which it is and why in Reasoning below.

The "helps other input shapes" case is the Optimizer's to make, not yours
to invent: assess the keep case it argued in `report.md` §5 (coverage +
other-shape rationale) and decide whether it holds. Do NOT manufacture a
rescue reason of your own to keep a change the Optimizer never justified —
if you suspect a real keep case it failed to argue, that is grounds for
ITERATE (send it back to substantiate), not a `keep` you author yourself.
-->

- **verdict:** ITERATE | PASS(target_reached | diminishing_returns | blocked)
- **confidence:** high | medium | low
- **code disposition (PASS only):** keep | revert-to-loop-baseline

